<--------------Singleton Design PAttern-------------------->

The Singleton design pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to that instance.

This is especially useful for situations where exactly one object is needed to coordinate actions across the system,
 such as database connections, logging services, resource management (eg thread pools).

 Private constructor: Prevents direct instantiation outside the class.
 Static member: Holds the single instance of the class.
 Public static method: Provides access to the single instance.


 ✅ What is Eager Initialization in Java?
 ans)Eager Initialization means creating the object at the time of class loading, even if it may never be used.\

 | Feature                | Eager            | Lazy                       |
 | ---------------------- | ---------------- | -------------------------- |
 | Creation Time          | At class loading | When requested first time  |
 | Thread-safe?           | Yes (by default) | No (needs synchronization) |
 | Memory Efficient?      | No               | Yes                        |
 | Performance on startup | Slightly slower  | Faster                     |
✅ What is Lazy Initialization?
Lazy Initialization means delaying the creation of an object until it's needed for the first time.

Unlike eager initialization (which creates the object when the class is loaded),
lazy initialization avoids creating the object unless it’s actually used.

❌ Problem in Multithreading
If two threads call getInstance() at the same time, two instances may be created.

